WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.491 --> 00:00:01.837
- I'm mad all the time.

00:00:01.837 --> 00:00:02.670
Mike can tell you

00:00:02.670 --> 00:00:05.047
Just angry, angry,
angry, pain in the neck.

00:00:05.047 --> 00:00:06.436
In the morning I would get
up maybe 45 minutes earlier

00:00:06.436 --> 00:00:10.603
to do meditation before I
was able to face that set.

00:00:11.867 --> 00:00:13.284
We create movies.

00:00:14.488 --> 00:00:16.811
Don't pay attention to
industry, do your own thing.

00:00:16.811 --> 00:00:17.944
Thinking in the morning,
we're going to do this

00:00:17.944 --> 00:00:20.414
then about two hours in, say we're doomed.

00:00:20.414 --> 00:00:21.247
(laughter)

00:00:21.247 --> 00:00:23.042
We're never finishing this film!

00:00:23.042 --> 00:00:24.307
We're going to be in movie hell

00:00:24.307 --> 00:00:26.543
for the rest of our lives.

00:00:26.543 --> 00:00:27.776
- He's an American director, producer,

00:00:27.776 --> 00:00:30.894
screenwriter and actor whose career spans

00:00:30.894 --> 00:00:33.041
more than 50 years.

00:00:33.041 --> 00:00:35.251
Part of the new Hollywood
wave of filmmaking,

00:00:35.251 --> 00:00:37.046
he's considered one of
the most influential

00:00:37.046 --> 00:00:39.680
and greatest filmmakers of all time.

00:00:39.680 --> 00:00:41.582
With 8 Best Director nominations,

00:00:41.582 --> 00:00:44.124
he's the most nominated living director.

00:00:44.124 --> 00:00:46.045
He's Martin Scorsese and here is my take

00:00:46.045 --> 00:00:48.005
on his top 10 rules for success.

00:00:48.005 --> 00:00:50.126
Rule number 7 is my personal
favorite and make sure

00:00:50.126 --> 00:00:51.723
to stick around all the
way through to the end

00:00:51.723 --> 00:00:53.269
for some special bonus clips.

00:00:53.269 --> 00:00:55.153
Also as you watch and listen
to Martin in these clips,

00:00:55.153 --> 00:00:56.168
if there's something that he says

00:00:56.168 --> 00:00:57.507
that really resonates with you,

00:00:57.507 --> 00:01:00.183
add it to the comments
below with quotation marks

00:01:00.183 --> 00:01:01.432
so we can keep track.

00:01:01.432 --> 00:01:04.408
(swoosh)
(thunk)

00:01:04.408 --> 00:01:06.385
- [Audience Member] I'm
wondering when you're trying

00:01:06.385 --> 00:01:08.450
to make a film and you
come up against obstacles

00:01:08.450 --> 00:01:12.432
and barriers, what do you
attribute your strong dedication

00:01:12.432 --> 00:01:14.599
to getting that film made?

00:01:17.162 --> 00:01:21.509
- Really, (laughs) hysteria, usually.

00:01:21.509 --> 00:01:22.399
(laughter)

00:01:22.399 --> 00:01:24.766
Hysteria and obsession and anger,

00:01:24.766 --> 00:01:26.254
I think mainly anger.

00:01:26.254 --> 00:01:27.569
(laughter)

00:01:27.569 --> 00:01:28.922
I'm mad all the time.

00:01:28.922 --> 00:01:29.755
Mike can tell you.

00:01:29.755 --> 00:01:32.270
Just angry, angry,
angry, pain in the neck.

00:01:32.270 --> 00:01:33.721
That's why I use humor a lot

00:01:33.721 --> 00:01:35.103
because it deflates myself

00:01:35.103 --> 00:01:36.579
I don't want to take it seriously

00:01:36.579 --> 00:01:38.945
because I really do.

00:01:38.945 --> 00:01:42.438
I would have been gone
much longer, 25 years ago.

00:01:42.438 --> 00:01:43.819
I wouldn't have survived.

00:01:43.819 --> 00:01:45.414
But the anger is enormous.

00:01:45.414 --> 00:01:48.086
About everything, about everything.

00:01:48.086 --> 00:01:51.186
So that also fuels me to
make a picture, I think.

00:01:51.186 --> 00:01:53.210
But keeping a sense of
humor is the key thing,

00:01:53.210 --> 00:01:55.664
the irony, the irony.

00:01:55.664 --> 00:01:56.836
When you get there in the morning

00:01:56.836 --> 00:02:00.458
and you get a note that one
actor's had a nervous breakdown

00:02:00.458 --> 00:02:03.208
and you an't go on with the film.

00:02:05.095 --> 00:02:06.163
(laughter)

00:02:06.163 --> 00:02:07.720
(applause)

00:02:07.720 --> 00:02:09.273
It happened, it happened.

00:02:09.273 --> 00:02:10.564
- [Host] They don't want to come out

00:02:10.564 --> 00:02:11.397
of the trailer.

00:02:11.397 --> 00:02:12.230
- Yeah, they don't want to
come out of the trailer.

00:02:12.230 --> 00:02:14.357
- Right here, right here
in the black sweater.

00:02:14.357 --> 00:02:15.841
What're you going to do?

00:02:15.841 --> 00:02:17.582
You get a heart attack otherwise.

00:02:17.582 --> 00:02:18.686
Or you kill someone.

00:02:18.686 --> 00:02:19.863
It's not good.

00:02:19.863 --> 00:02:21.962
- [Interviewer] Without
meaning to be presumptuous,

00:02:21.962 --> 00:02:25.184
what sacrifices do you
think you've made in order

00:02:25.184 --> 00:02:28.445
to create the work, to make
the films, make the career

00:02:28.445 --> 00:02:29.346
that you've done?

00:02:29.346 --> 00:02:31.100
What do you think in your
life you've sacrificed?

00:02:31.100 --> 00:02:33.819
- It think, as an old friend,
an old priest told me,

00:02:33.819 --> 00:02:35.617
said you pay a price.

00:02:35.617 --> 00:02:36.720
You pay a price.

00:02:36.720 --> 00:02:40.368
Just quite honestly, a
personal life, really.

00:02:40.368 --> 00:02:42.201
Relationships go by the board.

00:02:42.201 --> 00:02:44.951
It's as if the Frank Capra quote,

00:02:46.244 --> 00:02:49.478
it's like a drug or something
that you have to continue.

00:02:49.478 --> 00:02:51.680
Now, as I say, some
people call that drive,

00:02:51.680 --> 00:02:54.150
some call it obsession, some
just call it being selfish

00:02:54.150 --> 00:02:55.692
and being not a very nice person.

00:02:55.692 --> 00:02:58.833
But the reality is that I just had

00:02:58.833 --> 00:03:01.434
to continue that process.

00:03:01.434 --> 00:03:03.326
Whatta you do when you change

00:03:03.326 --> 00:03:05.909
how the world thinks of cinema?

00:03:08.380 --> 00:03:09.380
What's next?

00:03:12.851 --> 00:03:16.350
Do you keep making the same kind of film?

00:03:16.350 --> 00:03:19.983
Or if you're a person like Rossolini,

00:03:19.983 --> 00:03:23.608
if you are, you try
something experimental.

00:03:23.608 --> 00:03:25.784
You push further, and
that's just experimental

00:03:25.784 --> 00:03:29.738
for experiment's sake, but you
push the boundaries further.

00:03:29.738 --> 00:03:33.571
- Talk to me, talk to the group about

00:03:33.571 --> 00:03:36.927
why the subjects and the
difficulties in getting films made.

00:03:36.927 --> 00:03:40.095
Why can't you ever do
anything that's easy?

00:03:40.095 --> 00:03:43.236
- I guess any film is not really easy.

00:03:43.236 --> 00:03:44.403
I would think.

00:03:46.844 --> 00:03:49.401
I think you're driven by something

00:03:49.401 --> 00:03:52.297
that's like an obsession, I would think.

00:03:52.297 --> 00:03:53.785
Before you realize it, you have your eye

00:03:53.785 --> 00:03:56.624
through the eyepiece
and you say we're crazy.

00:03:56.624 --> 00:03:59.261
How the hell do we think
we're ever going to do this?

00:03:59.261 --> 00:04:01.690
This is madness, but you keep going.

00:04:01.690 --> 00:04:03.413
And I never really thought otherwise.

00:04:03.413 --> 00:04:07.620
I actually thought people
wanted to see that kind of film.

00:04:07.620 --> 00:04:11.787
The industry changed, I started
making Mean Streets in '73,

00:04:14.185 --> 00:04:16.699
Taxi Driver and Raging Bull
and New York, New York.

00:04:16.699 --> 00:04:20.163
Particularly in Last Waltz,
and Last Waltz was somewhat

00:04:20.163 --> 00:04:23.996
of a different story,
but I actually had no...

00:04:25.163 --> 00:04:26.682
I really was surprised

00:04:26.682 --> 00:04:27.937
that there would be resistance

00:04:27.937 --> 00:04:29.406
to Taxi Driver, for example.

00:04:29.406 --> 00:04:30.914
It was very difficult to get made.

00:04:30.914 --> 00:04:32.861
And certainly, Last Temptation
of Christ, I understood

00:04:32.861 --> 00:04:35.817
there would have been some controversy

00:04:35.817 --> 00:04:38.881
but usually from certain areas,

00:04:38.881 --> 00:04:42.381
not necessarily what happened at the time.

00:04:43.504 --> 00:04:45.087
So we just go ahead

00:04:46.007 --> 00:04:48.311
and eventually there's some studio people.

00:04:48.311 --> 00:04:50.174
In the case of Taxi Driver,
it was a combination

00:04:50.174 --> 00:04:51.645
of De Niro and myself.

00:04:51.645 --> 00:04:52.687
He'd just done Godfather II.

00:04:52.687 --> 00:04:55.563
When I did Mean Streets, I
brought the answer print up

00:04:55.563 --> 00:04:57.794
to show Francis Coppola in San Francisco

00:04:57.794 --> 00:05:01.255
and he hired De Niro for
Godfather II based on that.

00:05:01.255 --> 00:05:03.952
That led to Taxi Driver and
then Raging Bull was a project

00:05:03.952 --> 00:05:05.822
of De Niro's, really.

00:05:05.822 --> 00:05:08.989
I remember doing the film called Hugo,

00:05:10.342 --> 00:05:13.044
which was a film we
shot in three dimensions

00:05:13.044 --> 00:05:15.026
and it would take about
an hour to get there,

00:05:15.026 --> 00:05:16.121
to the studio.

00:05:16.121 --> 00:05:18.846
But in the morning I would get
up maybe 45 minutes earlier

00:05:18.846 --> 00:05:23.015
to do meditation before I
was able to face that set.

00:05:23.015 --> 00:05:25.208
(laughing)

00:05:25.208 --> 00:05:29.825
With children actors that could
only work for like a minute,

00:05:29.825 --> 00:05:30.907
(laughs) a dog that wasn't listening,

00:05:30.907 --> 00:05:33.260
Sacha Baron Cohen, who
was improvising everything

00:05:33.260 --> 00:05:35.010
and everything in 3D!

00:05:35.898 --> 00:05:37.593
And overscheduled and over budget!

00:05:37.593 --> 00:05:41.181
God, there's only one
thing you can do is...

00:05:41.181 --> 00:05:42.315
Calm it down.

00:05:42.315 --> 00:05:44.443
And get into it and
deal with the realities.

00:05:44.443 --> 00:05:45.846
If there is something on my mind,

00:05:45.846 --> 00:05:48.047
if there is something
I'm really worried about

00:05:48.047 --> 00:05:49.785
in terms of a character development

00:05:49.785 --> 00:05:52.535
or particularly, a thematic issue

00:05:55.777 --> 00:05:58.449
or just trying to get a shot done.

00:05:58.449 --> 00:06:00.173
Literally, they'd say
well, we can't do that,

00:06:00.173 --> 00:06:01.006
we don't have the hallway,

00:06:01.006 --> 00:06:02.329
there's got to be a track down a hallway.

00:06:02.329 --> 00:06:03.866
We have no hall.

00:06:03.866 --> 00:06:05.212
Now what?

00:06:05.212 --> 00:06:06.045
Well, what is it about?

00:06:06.045 --> 00:06:07.294
It's about his face.

00:06:07.294 --> 00:06:08.127
All right.

00:06:08.127 --> 00:06:09.195
And so you just forget it

00:06:09.195 --> 00:06:10.653
and you go into meditation.

00:06:10.653 --> 00:06:12.063
- That's it, and you
come up with an answer.

00:06:12.063 --> 00:06:13.442
- And somehow something came out.

00:06:13.442 --> 00:06:16.314
I said dammit, that's
(drowned out by laughter)

00:06:16.314 --> 00:06:19.472
I did my first short film at
the summer workshop in '63,

00:06:19.472 --> 00:06:20.698
that's where I met Thelma Schoonmaker.

00:06:20.698 --> 00:06:21.531
- Yeah.

00:06:21.531 --> 00:06:23.282
- She was a history major at Columbia.

00:06:23.282 --> 00:06:25.753
- Now, and Thelma
Schoonmaker is Marty's editor

00:06:25.753 --> 00:06:28.559
and this is, without overstating it,

00:06:28.559 --> 00:06:33.509
one of the truly great
collaborations in motion pictures.

00:06:33.509 --> 00:06:36.348
So for somebody who
has a vision like Marty

00:06:36.348 --> 00:06:38.835
to get other people, because
you've got to get other people

00:06:38.835 --> 00:06:41.915
to do it with you who are
on the same frequency,

00:06:41.915 --> 00:06:43.478
who understand what you're looking for,

00:06:43.478 --> 00:06:45.843
who share the vision
and can make it happen,

00:06:45.843 --> 00:06:46.936
that's priceless.

00:06:46.936 --> 00:06:49.293
- Yeah, it really is and
that's the struggle, too.

00:06:49.293 --> 00:06:50.330
Cause it is collaborative.

00:06:50.330 --> 00:06:52.032
If I could shoot it, and
if I could edit it myself

00:06:52.032 --> 00:06:53.969
and if I could write it,
if I could act in it, fine.

00:06:53.969 --> 00:06:54.817
But I can't.

00:06:54.817 --> 00:06:55.960
(laughing)

00:06:55.960 --> 00:06:58.100
So you need people who
are like-minded in a way

00:06:58.100 --> 00:07:00.680
or exploring certain
areas that you want to go

00:07:00.680 --> 00:07:03.020
to possibly at this
certain point in your life.

00:07:03.020 --> 00:07:04.857
But with Thelma and myself, we edit films

00:07:04.857 --> 00:07:06.123
just like I was saying, we're getting

00:07:06.123 --> 00:07:11.024
to be two old people in a cottage
making a three-piece suit.

00:07:11.024 --> 00:07:12.494
Sort of stitching it by hand.

00:07:12.494 --> 00:07:14.286
They're banging on the door of the studio,

00:07:14.286 --> 00:07:15.119
they want the picture.

00:07:15.119 --> 00:07:16.679
I'll be right with you,
just you got to put cuffs

00:07:16.679 --> 00:07:17.512
on the pants!

00:07:17.512 --> 00:07:18.444
That's it!

00:07:18.444 --> 00:07:20.951
But it's that sort of
thing, and there's a lot

00:07:20.951 --> 00:07:23.356
of care that goes into
it, and a lot of time

00:07:23.356 --> 00:07:25.942
that's spent in the actual
editing of the films.

00:07:25.942 --> 00:07:27.846
- Make your own industry.

00:07:27.846 --> 00:07:29.263
We create movies.

00:07:30.635 --> 00:07:34.802
Don't pay attention to
industry, do your own thing.

00:07:35.838 --> 00:07:37.171
Put it this way.

00:07:38.640 --> 00:07:41.054
You want the work to be seen

00:07:41.054 --> 00:07:44.182
but it doesn't have to be at the Odeon.

00:07:44.182 --> 00:07:48.583
No more, that's all different,
that's another ancient world.

00:07:48.583 --> 00:07:51.612
That type of film, or not
even that type of film,

00:07:51.612 --> 00:07:54.353
the communal experience
is always important.

00:07:54.353 --> 00:07:56.510
You can make a film on a camera the size

00:07:56.510 --> 00:07:58.286
of that doorknob and still show it

00:07:58.286 --> 00:08:01.343
to 1,600 people, an audience,

00:08:01.343 --> 00:08:03.750
and it's still a great
communal experience.

00:08:03.750 --> 00:08:06.851
It doesn't necessarily mean it has to cost

00:08:06.851 --> 00:08:09.509
over 100 million pounds.

00:08:09.509 --> 00:08:12.390
- Have you ever done a movie
where everything pretty much

00:08:12.390 --> 00:08:15.679
fell into place and there
were no monumental battles,

00:08:15.679 --> 00:08:18.960
no anxiety, no angst, no
near nervous breakdowns?

00:08:18.960 --> 00:08:21.886
Which seems to be actually kind
of what your life is about.

00:08:21.886 --> 00:08:22.719
- Pretty much, yeah.

00:08:22.719 --> 00:08:23.795
(laughing)

00:08:23.795 --> 00:08:25.545
- Has there ever been a
movie where you just started

00:08:25.545 --> 00:08:27.283
and what do we have, 45 days shooting?

00:08:27.283 --> 00:08:28.405
Did it like (snaps)?

00:08:28.405 --> 00:08:29.765
- No, never, never.

00:08:29.765 --> 00:08:30.938
(laughter)

00:08:30.938 --> 00:08:33.176
I mean I'm shooting at two
in the morning last night.

00:08:33.176 --> 00:08:36.134
You find yourself on the
edge of the seat, like this.

00:08:36.134 --> 00:08:36.967
Frozen.

00:08:36.967 --> 00:08:38.894
The actors are moving a
certain way, what is it?

00:08:38.894 --> 00:08:41.185
Maybe I think it should
be more to the left.

00:08:41.185 --> 00:08:42.658
Whatta you think, Michael Ballhaus?

00:08:42.658 --> 00:08:43.930
Yeah, move him more to his--

00:08:43.930 --> 00:08:45.402
I have a problem with left or right,

00:08:45.402 --> 00:08:46.235
so whenever I say left, I go (slaps) left!

00:08:46.235 --> 00:08:48.485
(laughter)

00:08:49.811 --> 00:08:50.998
No, right, that's it!

00:08:50.998 --> 00:08:52.537
And the guys are lookin at me.

00:08:52.537 --> 00:08:53.370
Marty?

00:08:53.370 --> 00:08:54.980
Oh, no, no, it's definitely to the left.

00:08:54.980 --> 00:08:56.201
But it's that kind of thing, you can't--

00:08:56.201 --> 00:08:57.690
Also as you get older, too

00:08:57.690 --> 00:08:59.892
sometimes with the heat on the set,

00:08:59.892 --> 00:09:01.727
and this week was very hard,

00:09:01.727 --> 00:09:05.883
you do find time to
get that energy midday.

00:09:05.883 --> 00:09:08.929
You need like 15 minutes,
close your eyes, just think.

00:09:08.929 --> 00:09:11.055
But you have to keep,
there's no such thing...

00:09:11.055 --> 00:09:12.789
People are "oh but you love shooting."

00:09:12.789 --> 00:09:14.891
For me shooting is an
extraordinarily stressful situation.

00:09:14.891 --> 00:09:17.134
Because there's not one moment to relax

00:09:17.134 --> 00:09:19.752
because you need to push,
you need to, first of all

00:09:19.752 --> 00:09:21.502
not only get something
wonderful from the actors

00:09:21.502 --> 00:09:24.005
and cinematographer and
everybody but you have

00:09:24.005 --> 00:09:26.187
to do it on a schedule.

00:09:26.187 --> 00:09:27.588
And you have to prove
that you're responsible.

00:09:27.588 --> 00:09:28.939
You have a certain responsibility.

00:09:28.939 --> 00:09:30.704
$96 million dollars, $100 million dollars.

00:09:30.704 --> 00:09:32.013
Yeah, you have a responsibility

00:09:32.013 --> 00:09:33.648
to do what you can with that money.

00:09:33.648 --> 00:09:34.918
You have to present a picture.

00:09:34.918 --> 00:09:36.827
You have to make the day.
- Make the day, means you get

00:09:36.827 --> 00:09:39.899
to the end, you've done
the set-ups and the pages,

00:09:39.899 --> 00:09:40.847
that's the way they judge these things.

00:09:40.847 --> 00:09:42.313
- Yeah and then you usually feel sometimes

00:09:42.313 --> 00:09:43.263
well, I'm not shooting this schedule

00:09:43.263 --> 00:09:44.599
for God's sakes, you're making a film!

00:09:44.599 --> 00:09:45.432
But then you realize well,

00:09:45.432 --> 00:09:48.700
at a certain point
sometimes you slip behind.

00:09:48.700 --> 00:09:51.637
We were able to, for example,
on Aviator, that picture

00:09:51.637 --> 00:09:52.470
came in on schedule.

00:09:52.470 --> 00:09:54.502
90 days, on schedule, all that stuff

00:09:54.502 --> 00:09:55.819
with the airplanes and everything.

00:09:55.819 --> 00:09:56.652
I don't know how we did it,

00:09:56.652 --> 00:09:58.870
but there wasn't one day
that we didn't suffer

00:09:58.870 --> 00:10:00.453
kind of the ups and downs of it.

00:10:00.453 --> 00:10:01.628
Thinking in the morning,
we're going to do this,

00:10:01.628 --> 00:10:03.768
then about two hours into
it, say we're doomed.

00:10:03.768 --> 00:10:04.601
(laughter)

00:10:04.601 --> 00:10:06.667
We're never finishing this film!

00:10:06.667 --> 00:10:10.263
We're going to be in movie
hell for the rest of our lives.

00:10:10.263 --> 00:10:12.928
The planes, Howard Hughes, I
don't know what's going on.

00:10:12.928 --> 00:10:13.865
Where are we?

00:10:13.865 --> 00:10:15.152
Then two hours later, hey!

00:10:15.152 --> 00:10:18.558
By the end of the day, it
was my God, we made it.

00:10:18.558 --> 00:10:20.176
Or you slipped over to the next day

00:10:20.176 --> 00:10:21.313
and cut down another scene

00:10:21.313 --> 00:10:22.888
and somehow it all came together.

00:10:22.888 --> 00:10:23.721
- Yeah.

00:10:23.721 --> 00:10:26.002
- By the time the dust had
settled, we looked around,

00:10:26.002 --> 00:10:28.462
it was 90 days, we did it.

00:10:28.462 --> 00:10:29.755
In Gangs, it was a different thing.

00:10:29.755 --> 00:10:31.033
It was a much bigger effort,

00:10:31.033 --> 00:10:32.756
you're looking around like we never did,

00:10:32.756 --> 00:10:34.840
we just never got there.

00:10:34.840 --> 00:10:36.907
But that's the last of
that kind that can be done.

00:10:36.907 --> 00:10:38.645
- [Audience Member] What's the
hallmark of a great script?

00:10:38.645 --> 00:10:40.509
How do you know you
want to choose that one

00:10:40.509 --> 00:10:43.239
over thousands or hundreds
of others that you look at?

00:10:43.239 --> 00:10:44.072
What--

00:10:44.072 --> 00:10:48.239
- Personally, usually I don't
read other people's scripts.

00:10:49.841 --> 00:10:52.908
I usually develop something
or I have something in mind.

00:10:52.908 --> 00:10:55.618
I work with a friend, usually,

00:10:55.618 --> 00:10:58.671
in some cases Nick Pileggi,
in some cases Jay Cocks

00:10:58.671 --> 00:11:01.297
or hire Paul Schrader for
Bringing Out The Dead.

00:11:01.297 --> 00:11:03.228
But, for example, when
Taxi Driver was given

00:11:03.228 --> 00:11:05.895
to me to read by Brian De Palma,

00:11:06.789 --> 00:11:08.846
I hadn't done Mean Streets yet.

00:11:08.846 --> 00:11:10.627
When he said "you'll be great for this,"

00:11:10.627 --> 00:11:14.086
I automatically, it had to
be an emotional connection

00:11:14.086 --> 00:11:16.828
to the material and to the characters.

00:11:16.828 --> 00:11:19.073
The obsessiveness of Travis Bickle,

00:11:19.073 --> 00:11:20.570
the sense of being the outsider

00:11:20.570 --> 00:11:23.191
and not being the dispossessed.

00:11:23.191 --> 00:11:25.988
We felt that way, myself and I could speak

00:11:25.988 --> 00:11:27.066
for De Niro to a certain extent

00:11:27.066 --> 00:11:28.692
because we didn't articulate it that way,

00:11:28.692 --> 00:11:31.415
I can't really say oh, we're outsiders.

00:11:31.415 --> 00:11:32.847
We were tryin' to get in!

00:11:32.847 --> 00:11:33.680
(Martin drowns out audience member)

00:11:33.680 --> 00:11:35.054
We're tryin' to stay in
Hollywood and in the meantime,

00:11:35.054 --> 00:11:36.019
this thing is--

00:11:36.019 --> 00:11:38.772
- [Host] Few of the
films came from scripts.

00:11:38.772 --> 00:11:41.715
Most of Marty's works
come from ideas and books

00:11:41.715 --> 00:11:43.074
and he then develops scripts.

00:11:43.074 --> 00:11:44.916
In the early years, there
were some screenplays

00:11:44.916 --> 00:11:46.197
but as you progressed on--

00:11:46.197 --> 00:11:49.346
- Yeah, Alice was a screenplay
but certainly Taxi Driver,

00:11:49.346 --> 00:11:52.247
when you got that, I said
this one has to be made.

00:11:52.247 --> 00:11:54.021
And we just knew it.

00:11:54.021 --> 00:11:55.729
But after that, no.

00:11:55.729 --> 00:11:59.195
After that,the world
developed the way Mike said.

00:11:59.195 --> 00:12:01.872
I think, yes, yes, I think so.

00:12:01.872 --> 00:12:04.092
New York Stories, for
example, Life Lessons

00:12:04.092 --> 00:12:06.825
with Nick Nolte, that was based on,

00:12:06.825 --> 00:12:08.825
I'd always wanted to do
something with Dostoevsky

00:12:08.825 --> 00:12:11.091
because actually the
first Dostoevsky I read

00:12:11.091 --> 00:12:12.154
was Notes From Underground.

00:12:12.154 --> 00:12:13.413
That was Taxi Driver, really.

00:12:13.413 --> 00:12:15.590
(laughter)

00:12:15.590 --> 00:12:17.099
So...

00:12:17.099 --> 00:12:19.063
I said hey, I feel like that, that's me,

00:12:19.063 --> 00:12:21.889
that sort of thing, so you
really identify with it.

00:12:21.889 --> 00:12:24.092
- You know who was on here the other day?

00:12:24.092 --> 00:12:25.298
The kid, Jonah--

00:12:25.298 --> 00:12:26.546
- Oh, Jonah Hill!

00:12:26.546 --> 00:12:28.633
- And he was talking about
a scene where he's got to

00:12:28.633 --> 00:12:30.417
take a punch from a thug.

00:12:30.417 --> 00:12:31.250
- Mm-hmm.

00:12:31.250 --> 00:12:33.246
- And it was a fake punch,
and he was going to pretend

00:12:33.246 --> 00:12:34.986
to be hit and you went over

00:12:34.986 --> 00:12:37.139
and said to him "we're
really going to hit ya."

00:12:37.139 --> 00:12:38.018
- Yes.

00:12:38.018 --> 00:12:39.571
No, at that point, I had to do it.

00:12:39.571 --> 00:12:40.404
- Yeah, of course you did
- He was getting on my nerves

00:12:40.404 --> 00:12:41.784
and that was the end of it.

00:12:41.784 --> 00:12:42.976
(laughs)

00:12:42.976 --> 00:12:44.298
Jonah could get you crazy!

00:12:44.298 --> 00:12:45.747
(applause)

00:12:45.747 --> 00:12:48.164
He's not going to stop flappin'!

00:12:49.387 --> 00:12:50.460
It's in the film.

00:12:50.460 --> 00:12:52.719
- He was actually knocked unconscious!

00:12:52.719 --> 00:12:54.921
- Actually, no, he pretended that.

00:12:54.921 --> 00:12:56.945
For a moment there, I thought he was.

00:12:56.945 --> 00:12:57.778
- No.

00:12:57.778 --> 00:12:58.611
- I said oh, maybe we went too far.

00:12:58.611 --> 00:12:59.869
- Yeah, maybe you went too far.

00:12:59.869 --> 00:13:03.383
Are there other actors
you've hurt in your career?

00:13:03.383 --> 00:13:04.216
- Joe Pesci.

00:13:04.216 --> 00:13:05.499
- Joe Pesci.

00:13:05.499 --> 00:13:06.677
(laughter)

00:13:06.677 --> 00:13:08.594
What did you do to him?

00:13:09.711 --> 00:13:10.803
- I didn't do it.

00:13:10.803 --> 00:13:14.208
It was all the fight scenes
and all the craziness

00:13:14.208 --> 00:13:16.853
in that film Raging Bull,
all the physicality.

00:13:16.853 --> 00:13:18.853
The last two days of
shooting or something,

00:13:18.853 --> 00:13:21.286
we did a scene with De Niro and Pesci

00:13:21.286 --> 00:13:22.869
in the boxing ring.

00:13:23.756 --> 00:13:26.528
There was a gym on 14th
street, which is gone now.

00:13:26.528 --> 00:13:28.997
And they had protective gear on.

00:13:28.997 --> 00:13:30.165
This is one of the strangest things.

00:13:30.165 --> 00:13:31.553
We see these action films, I don't know

00:13:31.553 --> 00:13:33.410
how people don't really get badly hurt.

00:13:33.410 --> 00:13:36.016
Because with the protective gear,

00:13:36.016 --> 00:13:38.181
there were a couple of punches thrown

00:13:38.181 --> 00:13:41.520
and the last punch,
just as I yelled "cut",

00:13:41.520 --> 00:13:43.971
Bob just hit, by accident really,

00:13:43.971 --> 00:13:47.131
the protective gear on the chest of Joe

00:13:47.131 --> 00:13:49.397
and we heard this (mmph).

00:13:49.397 --> 00:13:50.959
I said "wow, that was great,"

00:13:50.959 --> 00:13:52.688
I said "what was that?"

00:13:52.688 --> 00:13:54.362
That was a broken rib!

00:13:54.362 --> 00:13:56.122
(laughter)

00:13:56.122 --> 00:13:58.208
He really broke and cracked his rib

00:13:58.208 --> 00:14:00.006
and it was just by accident with gear on.

00:14:00.006 --> 00:14:01.109
And that's in the film, too.

00:14:01.109 --> 00:14:02.564
That sound is there.

00:14:02.564 --> 00:14:03.699
(laughter)

00:14:03.699 --> 00:14:05.382
And then we had the opportunity

00:14:05.382 --> 00:14:07.789
to revisit it again on Casino.

00:14:07.789 --> 00:14:08.858
- The same--

00:14:08.858 --> 00:14:10.027
- The same rib!

00:14:10.027 --> 00:14:10.860
- No!

00:14:10.860 --> 00:14:11.693
- Yes!

00:14:11.693 --> 00:14:12.951
(applause)

00:14:12.951 --> 00:14:13.936
- How did this happen?

00:14:13.936 --> 00:14:14.977
- Well, he was on the floor

00:14:14.977 --> 00:14:16.186
and it was a big guy holding him down.

00:14:16.186 --> 00:14:18.010
Every time I'd say "cut" this big guy,

00:14:18.010 --> 00:14:20.346
who was like 200 pounds,
would sort of use Joe

00:14:20.346 --> 00:14:21.723
as a hassock or something.

00:14:21.723 --> 00:14:24.279
And Joe said "don't do it,
don't use me as a hassock,

00:14:24.279 --> 00:14:25.521
"how many times I got to tell you?"

00:14:25.521 --> 00:14:26.942
And sure enough, crack.

00:14:26.942 --> 00:14:27.775
The same rib.

00:14:27.775 --> 00:14:29.522
(laughs)

00:14:29.522 --> 00:14:30.854
- You're lucky you're still in business.

00:14:30.854 --> 00:14:31.692
- Yes, I sure am.

00:14:31.692 --> 00:14:33.775
(laughs)

00:14:36.039 --> 00:14:37.289
- Thank you guys so much for watching.

00:14:37.289 --> 00:14:38.514
I hope you enjoyed.

00:14:38.514 --> 00:14:41.399
I made this for you because
len-ul-vah-tu-nin asked me to.

00:14:41.399 --> 00:14:42.573
So if there's a famous entrepreneur

00:14:42.573 --> 00:14:44.216
that you want me to profile next,

00:14:44.216 --> 00:14:45.710
leave it down in the comments below

00:14:45.710 --> 00:14:46.797
and I'll see what I can do.

00:14:46.797 --> 00:14:47.630
I'd also love to know which

00:14:47.630 --> 00:14:49.748
of Martin's top 10 rules
had the biggest impact

00:14:49.748 --> 00:14:51.055
on you and why.

00:14:51.055 --> 00:14:52.468
What made you reflect?

00:14:52.468 --> 00:14:54.936
What really touched your soul in some way?

00:14:54.936 --> 00:14:56.097
Leave it in the comments

00:14:56.097 --> 00:14:57.263
and I will join the discussion.

00:14:57.263 --> 00:15:00.135
Also, if any of his quotes
that have been posted below

00:15:00.135 --> 00:15:02.575
that you see in quotation
marks that you really enjoyed,

00:15:02.575 --> 00:15:03.767
make sure to give it a thumbs-up

00:15:03.767 --> 00:15:05.069
to show your appreciation.

00:15:05.069 --> 00:15:07.310
Finally, I wanted to give a
quick shout-out to Neeta Singh.

00:15:07.310 --> 00:15:09.000
Neeta, thank you so much
for buying two copies

00:15:09.000 --> 00:15:11.234
of my book, it really means a lot to me.

00:15:11.234 --> 00:15:13.367
For those of you watching,
if you want your chance

00:15:13.367 --> 00:15:14.698
at a shot in a future video, make sure

00:15:14.698 --> 00:15:17.387
to grab a copy of the book and also email

00:15:17.387 --> 00:15:19.099
in your receipt, so we can keep track.

00:15:19.099 --> 00:15:20.953
Thank you guys so much for watching.

00:15:20.953 --> 00:15:23.571
Continue to believe or
whatever your one word is.

00:15:23.571 --> 00:15:25.177
And I'll see you soon.

00:15:25.177 --> 00:15:29.260
(swoosh)
(thump)

00:15:31.008 --> 00:15:31.841
(applause)

00:15:31.841 --> 00:15:33.386
- This was Schrader's passion, in a way,

00:15:33.386 --> 00:15:34.915
and then somehow we picked up on it.

00:15:34.915 --> 00:15:35.983
It was De Niro and myself and we all felt

00:15:35.983 --> 00:15:39.224
as powerfully attached
to it as Schrader did.

00:15:39.224 --> 00:15:41.605
New York New York came from
something that I wanted

00:15:41.605 --> 00:15:43.558
to do and explore genre, and
I started fooling around,

00:15:43.558 --> 00:15:46.504
mixing genre with what you might call

00:15:46.504 --> 00:15:48.907
the improvisatory style,
like the Cassavetes

00:15:48.907 --> 00:15:50.770
or even Cussen to a certain extent

00:15:50.770 --> 00:15:52.533
and blending the two.

00:15:52.533 --> 00:15:54.733
That was a failure at the box office

00:15:54.733 --> 00:15:56.150
and in Hollywood.

00:15:57.295 --> 00:15:58.548
It sort of set me off on a road,

00:15:58.548 --> 00:16:02.563
feeling I never knew if
I had the passion again

00:16:02.563 --> 00:16:04.931
to do something from myself.

00:16:04.931 --> 00:16:06.250
- Was it to make a movie again?

00:16:06.250 --> 00:16:07.083
- To make a movie.

00:16:07.083 --> 00:16:09.625
To ever make a movie again
that came from in here

00:16:09.625 --> 00:16:12.948
because I realized that I
just couldn't go to work.

00:16:12.948 --> 00:16:15.013
Meaning, go to work like
okay,here's the script,

00:16:15.013 --> 00:16:17.570
go there Monday morning, be there at 5:30

00:16:17.570 --> 00:16:19.820
and tell this person and that person

00:16:19.820 --> 00:16:20.723
and the actor will have his own ideas

00:16:20.723 --> 00:16:21.694
or her own ideas and you don't agree

00:16:21.694 --> 00:16:23.474
with them, but you're
going to have to go--

00:16:23.474 --> 00:16:24.779
I don't know if I could do that.

00:16:24.779 --> 00:16:25.871
I don't know if I have the energy to do it

00:16:25.871 --> 00:16:27.559
or really wanted to do it.

00:16:27.559 --> 00:16:29.939
So De Niro, during the period
of making New York New York

00:16:29.939 --> 00:16:30.772
and a little earlier, actually,

00:16:30.772 --> 00:16:32.455
when I was doing Alice
Doesn't Live Here Anymore,

00:16:32.455 --> 00:16:33.429
he gave me the book of Raging Bull,

00:16:33.429 --> 00:16:35.747
he wanted to make this.

00:16:35.747 --> 00:16:38.414
By that point in time, it seemed

00:16:39.417 --> 00:16:41.325
to be more generated from him.

00:16:41.325 --> 00:16:42.594
As I said, sports films I didn't get.

00:16:42.594 --> 00:16:44.074
But it's not a sports
film, I didn't realize that

00:16:44.074 --> 00:16:46.241
until I'd gone through
my own two or three years

00:16:46.241 --> 00:16:50.821
of soul-searching and
having some difficult times

00:16:50.821 --> 00:16:52.597
and finally winding up in the hospital.

00:16:52.597 --> 00:16:55.223
I found that ultimately,
when I was in the hospital,

00:16:55.223 --> 00:16:59.717
it was kind of like a
strange miracle in a way.

00:16:59.717 --> 00:17:02.962
I couldn't reach any further down.

00:17:02.962 --> 00:17:04.329
I was at bottom.

00:17:04.329 --> 00:17:06.537
When he came to see me,
and he just wanted really

00:17:06.537 --> 00:17:07.747
to make that film, he
wanted to make me get out

00:17:07.747 --> 00:17:08.610
of that hospital bed, too, but he wanted

00:17:08.610 --> 00:17:09.443
to make the film.

00:17:09.443 --> 00:17:11.130
(laughter)

00:17:11.130 --> 00:17:12.234
He said come on, you can do this!

00:17:12.234 --> 00:17:14.074
You'd be so great and what are you doing

00:17:14.074 --> 00:17:15.119
with your life?

00:17:15.119 --> 00:17:16.335
Look at you!

00:17:16.335 --> 00:17:17.953
Come on, please, you can really do it.

00:17:17.953 --> 00:17:19.099
And I said okay.

00:17:19.099 --> 00:17:21.181
(laughter)

00:17:21.181 --> 00:17:22.867
It suddenly hit me, I think I understood

00:17:22.867 --> 00:17:24.545
what it was and somehow through him,

00:17:24.545 --> 00:17:28.488
we had this extraordinary collaboration.

00:17:28.488 --> 00:17:31.092
Mostly, in a sense,
very very collaborative

00:17:31.092 --> 00:17:34.399
and almost to the point where
we didn't necessarily have

00:17:34.399 --> 00:17:36.807
to talk, that we kind of felt similar ways

00:17:36.807 --> 00:17:39.140
about life and about people.

00:17:40.429 --> 00:17:42.785
The process, the artistic
process, and just living,

00:17:42.785 --> 00:17:43.924
trying to live.

00:17:43.924 --> 00:17:45.846
Being, and all these different emotions

00:17:45.846 --> 00:17:47.846
we felt we kind of shared.

00:17:47.846 --> 00:17:49.507
I thought that was the film.

